# Binary assets first, and explicitly. Exo/wwwroot holds the built UI bundle *and*
# the game/module logos and webfonts. A blanket rule over that directory would hand
# PNG and WOFF bytes to git's line-ending filter, which rewrites any 0x0D 0x0A pair
# inside them and quietly corrupts the file.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.dll binary
*.exe binary
*.asar binary
*.nip binary
*.bin binary
*.snapshot binary

# Vite writes the committed bundle with LF. This repo is developed on Windows with
# core.autocrlf=true, which checks those text files out as CRLF — so every
# `npm run build` rewrote every line and made Exo/wwwroot show as fully modified,
# burying the one asset hash that actually changed under whole-file diffs.
Exo/wwwroot/index.html text eol=lf
# The Vite template feeds wwwroot/index.html; without the same rule the source
# checks out as CRLF under autocrlf=true and the build rewrites the shipped file.
ui/index.html text eol=lf
Exo/wwwroot/**/*.js text eol=lf
Exo/wwwroot/**/*.css text eol=lf
Exo/wwwroot/**/*.svg text eol=lf

# The shipped PowerShell kits are SHA-256 hashed into ShippedScriptManifest.g.cs.
# The manifest generator, the runtime verifier, and Ui.Smoke all canonicalise CRLF to
# LF before hashing (Generate-ScriptManifest.ps1, ShippedScriptManifest.cs), so
# integrity does not depend on checkout policy. Recorded here so the next person does
# not have to rediscover why that normalisation exists before touching it.
*.ps1 text

# .editorconfig sets end_of_line = crlf and `dotnet format` gates it, but nothing
# pinned the checkout: AmdNativeApply.cs and HardwareInventory.cs entered the index
# as LF and stayed LF in the working tree, so `dotnet format --verify-no-changes`
# was red with 511 ENDOFLINE errors in files nobody had touched. The index still
# stores LF; this only fixes what lands on disk, on every platform.
*.cs text eol=crlf
